Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `riis-portfolio`. Clone it. Open it in Visual Studio Code.

Install the dependencies.

```bash
npm install
```

Start the dev server.

```bash
npm run dev
```

Open [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). You'll see the article-style demo page our template ships with — the same one we've been recycling all semester.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Delete everything. We're going to start from a blank slate.

Save and check your browser. The page will be blank, except for the header and footer.

We want to delete those too, so let's go into the `+page.js` file and set `showHeader` and `showFooter` to `false`:

```js
export const load = () => {
  return {
    showHeader: false,
    showFooter: false
  };
};
```

Now you should have a completely blank page. This is our canvas.

As in past weeks, your class template already includes the components you need. Like the rest of the library, they're documented on the [Storybook](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/) site.

In the compositions section, you can see a pre-built Portfolio page that shows how the new [Profile](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/portfolio-profile--docs) component and the [Card](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/?path=/docs/data-card--docs) component we've used in past weeks work together. The example is a portfolio for [Max Eastman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Eastman), a controversial editor from the early 20th century who lived in Greenwich Village and published a magazine called [The Masses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Masses).

