## Part 5: Adding the clip grid

The profile is done. Now we need the second half of the homepage: a grid of cards showing Riis's work.

### Adding clips to the YAML

Open `content.yaml` and add a `clips` section below the profile. For now, each clip only needs the fields that appear on the card — we'll add more later when we build the detail pages.

```yaml
clips:
  - slug: police
    title: Police Headquarters Reporter
    image: /thumbnails/police.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Street police headquarters in the late 1880s
    description: A decade of police-beat reporting at 300 Mulberry Street for the New-York Tribune and the Evening Sun, covering crime, immigration, and the daily life of the Lower East Side.

  - slug: how-the-other-half
    title: How the Other Half Lives
    image: /thumbnails/bandits-roost.jpg
    imageAlt: Bandit's Roost, a Riis photograph of an alley off Mulberry Street, 1888
    description: A book-length investigation of New York tenement life, illustrated with my own flash photographs of the city after dark. Published by Charles Scribner's Sons in 1890.

  - slug: mulberry-bend
    title: The Battle for Mulberry Bend
    image: /thumbnails/mulberry-bend.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Bend before demolition, photographed in the early 1890s
    description: A sustained reporting and advocacy effort to demolish Mulberry Bend, the most notorious slum block in lower Manhattan, and replace it with a public park. The block came down in 1894. Columbus Park stands there today.
```

Save thumbnail images to `static/thumbnails/police.jpg`, `bandits-roost.jpg`, and `mulberry-bend.jpg` to match the paths above. Riis's photographs are public domain and widely available — try the [Library of Congress Jacob Riis collection](https://www.loc.gov/collections/jacob-riis/), the [Museum of the City of New York](https://collections.mcny.org/), or [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Photographs_by_Jacob_Riis).

### Rendering the card grid

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Import the Card component and add the grid below the Profile:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3,4,9-18"}
<script>
  import Profile from '$lib/components/Portfolio/Profile.svelte';
  import Card from '$lib/components/Card.svelte';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';

  let { data } = $props();
  const content = data.content;
</script>

<div class="container wide">
  <Profile
    name={content.profile.name}
    tagline={content.profile.tagline}
    photo={content.profile.photo}
    photoAlt={content.profile.photoAlt}
    email={content.profile.email}
    github={content.profile.github}
    linkedin={content.profile.linkedin}
    bio={content.profile.bio}
  />

  <div class="card-grid">
    {#each content.clips as clip (clip.title)}
      <Card href="{base}/clips/{clip.slug}" image={clip.image} imageAlt={clip.title}>
        <h3>{clip.title}</h3>
        <p>{clip.description}</p>
      </Card>
    {/each}
  </div>
</div>
```

The Card component handles the tile layout and the link wrapping. The `card-grid` class handles the responsive arrangement — wide screens get two or three cards per row, phones collapse to a single column. We don't have to write any CSS for either.

Save and check your browser. The Riis profile should appear up top, with three clip cards in a grid below it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-12/homepage-real-data.png"
  alt="The Riis portfolio homepage now showing Jacob Riis as the subject with his portrait, tagline, now/next paragraph, and three clip cards with real Riis photographs as thumbnails — all loaded from the YAML data file"
  chromeTitle="Jacob Riis"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's the homepage done. Every word that shows up on it lives in `content.yaml`. Want to change the tagline? Edit one line of YAML. Want to add a fourth clip? Add a fourth entry to the list. The Svelte code stays put.

Now we need a place for each card to lead.

## Part 6: Clip detail pages

Every card on the homepage links to `/clips/[slug]` — a URL that doesn't exist yet. Click any of them and you'll get a 404. Let's fix that.

You've seen this pattern before. Back in [Week 9](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-9), when we built the Bronx Housing Violations database explorer, every building had its own page at `/building/[id]`. We didn't create a separate folder for each building. We created one dynamic route — a folder named `[id]` in square brackets — and SvelteKit matched any URL with the right shape and ran a load function to find the matching record.

We're going to do the same thing here, but with `[slug]` and YAML.

### Adding detail fields to the YAML

Each detail page needs more than what the card shows. Open `content.yaml` and add three new fields to each clip: `url`, `skills`, and `body`. Your clips section should now look like this:

```yaml
clips:
  - slug: police
    title: Police Headquarters Reporter
    image: /thumbnails/police.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Street police headquarters in the late 1880s
    description: A decade of police-beat reporting at 300 Mulberry Street for the New-York Tribune and the Evening Sun, covering crime, immigration, and the daily life of the Lower East Side.
    url: https://www.loc.gov/collections/jacob-riis/
    skills:
      - Late-night police reporting
      - Source-building across language barriers
      - Working with the city coroner's records
      - Daily news pace
    body: |
      The police beat is where I learned the city. Mulberry Street headquarters sits on the edge of the worst tenements in New York, and every story I covered as a reporter — every arrest, every fire, every dead body — pulled me deeper into the lives of the people on the other side of the door.

      I didn't yet know I would write a book about what I was seeing. But I was learning the geography, the families, and the patterns. The reform work that came later would have been impossible without the reporting work that came first.

  - slug: how-the-other-half
    title: How the Other Half Lives
    image: /thumbnails/bandits-roost.jpg
    imageAlt: Bandit's Roost, a Riis photograph of an alley off Mulberry Street, 1888
    description: A book-length investigation of New York tenement life, illustrated with my own flash photographs of the city after dark. Published by Charles Scribner's Sons in 1890.
    url: https://www.loc.gov/item/06017839/
    skills:
      - Magnesium-flash photography
      - Long-form documentary writing
      - Lantern-slide lecturing
      - Reporting across class lines
    body: |
      The flash powder gave me the picture, but the picture is only half the work. The other half is the words — telling readers in the brownstones uptown what the inside of a Five Points tenement smells like, sounds like, who lives there and how they came to be there.

      The book sold well enough that even Theodore Roosevelt came to find me. He left a note on my door saying he had read the book and had come to help. That was the moment I understood what reporting could do.

  - slug: mulberry-bend
    title: The Battle for Mulberry Bend
    image: /thumbnails/mulberry-bend.jpg
    imageAlt: Mulberry Bend before demolition, photographed in the early 1890s
    description: A sustained reporting and advocacy effort to demolish Mulberry Bend, the most notorious slum block in lower Manhattan, and replace it with a public park. The block came down in 1894. Columbus Park stands there today.
    url: https://www.mcny.org/
    skills:
      - Sustained-focus accountability reporting
      - Working with city government and reform organizations
      - Combining photography, writing, and public lectures
      - Patience
    body: |
      The Bend was three acres of the foulest tenement block in the city — narrow alleys where the sun never reached, buildings that should have been condemned a generation earlier, and a death rate among the children many times higher than the city average.

      I wrote about it for years. I photographed it. I lectured on it. Reform is slow. The Bend went from a block I first reported on as a young man to a public park nearly twenty years later.
```

Notice how the multiline `body` fields render cleanly in your editor — no escaped quotes, no `\n\n`, just paragraphs separated by blank lines. That's the payoff for using YAML block scalars.

### The load function

Create a new folder at `src/routes/clips/[slug]/` — the square brackets make it a dynamic route, just like `[id]` in Week 9. Then add `+page.js` inside it:

```js
import content from '$lib/data/content.yaml';
import { error } from '@sveltejs/kit';

export const load = ({ params }) => {
  const clip = content.clips.find((c) => c.slug === params.slug);

  if (!clip) {
    error(404, 'Clip not found');
  }

  return {
    name: content.profile.name,
    detail: clip
  };
};
```

This does three things:

1. Imports the same YAML file the homepage loads from. One source of truth for the whole site.
2. Finds the record inside `content.clips` whose `slug` matches the URL parameter. The `params` object is populated by SvelteKit based on the folder name — `[slug]` becomes `params.slug`.
3. If no record matches, throws a 404. Otherwise, hands the clip record to the page as `detail`, along with the profile `name` for the kicker line.

### Rendering the detail page

We have the data flowing into the page. Now we need to render it. This time we'll compose the page directly from template components rather than building a custom one.

Open `src/routes/clips/[slug]/+page.svelte` and paste in:

```svelte
<script>
  import Kicker from '$lib/components/Article/Kicker.svelte';
  import Headline from '$lib/components/Article/Headline.svelte';
  import Image from '$lib/components/Media/Image.svelte';
  import Rule from '$lib/components/Layout/Rule.svelte';
  import TagList from '$lib/components/Data/TagList.svelte';
  import ArticleBody from '$lib/components/Article/ArticleBody.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
</script>

<div class="container">
<Kicker text="{data.name}'s Portfolio" />
<Headline text={data.detail.title} />

<Image src={data.detail.image} alt={data.detail.title} />

<Rule />
<TagList label="Skills" tags={data.detail.skills} />
<TagList
  label="View Project"
  tags={[{ text: new URL(data.detail.url).hostname, href: data.detail.url }]}
/>
<Rule />

<ArticleBody>
  {#each data.detail.body.trim().split('\n\n') as paragraph, i (i)}
    <p>{paragraph}</p>
  {/each}
</ArticleBody>
</div>
```

No custom component file, no CSS to write. Each template component handles its own styling. The `Kicker` shows the subject's name as a breadcrumb-style label above the headline. The two `TagList` components display the skills as pills and the project link as a clickable tag — `new URL(data.detail.url).hostname` extracts just the domain name for a clean label. The `ArticleBody` wraps the prose paragraphs in the same readable column width we've used all semester.

The `.split('\n\n')` call is the payoff for using YAML block scalars: prose written naturally in the data file renders as proper `<p>` tags on the page.

Save and click into one of the cards on your homepage. You should see a full detail page.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-12/project-detail.png"
  alt="A clip detail page showing the kicker, headline, hero image, skills as tag pills, a link to the original work, and the body prose — all composed from template components"
  chromeTitle="How the Other Half Lives"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/clips/how-the-other-half"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Click the kicker or use your browser's back button to return to the homepage. Click into another clip. Same template components, different content. All from one YAML file.

## Part 7: Deploy and beyond

When you're ready, commit and push to GitHub. Watch the Actions tab to make sure the build succeeds.

Once your site is live, share the URL in the class chat. Pull up a few of your classmates' Riis sites. See how differently everyone treated the same source material.

## Homework

Tonight you built Riis's portfolio with me. Now you'll build one of your own.

### Task 1: Pick your subject

You have two options.

**Option A: Build it for yourself.** Replace Riis with you. Edit `content.yaml` so the profile section holds your real name, tagline, photo, contact info, and now/next paragraph, and the clips section holds three of your own clips — your three Module 2 builds are the obvious choice, but you can include outside work if you have it. The result should be something you'd actually send to an editor.

**Option B: Build it for a different historical journalist.** Pick a real reporter, columnist, or correspondent from the past and build their portfolio the way we built Riis's. Some candidates: Ida B. Wells, Ida Tarbell, Lincoln Steffens, Nellie Bly, Dorothy Thompson, Ernie Pyle, Willa Cather, John Reed, Gay Talese, William F. Buckley. Pick three of their major works, write the YAML, and find public-domain images to illustrate.

Whichever option you pick, the technical requirements are the same: every field on every clip filled in, every Profile field populated where appropriate, every link working, the site deployed to GitHub Pages.

### Task 2: Make it look like the subject

The default styles are fine, but they're generic. Spend real time making the site feel like it belongs to its subject — whether that's you, Riis if you want to keep going, or whoever you picked for Option B.

Some directions to play with:

- **Typography.** Swap the default font for something with personality. [Google Fonts](https://fonts.google.com/) is the easiest place to browse. A serif display face with a clean sans for body text is a classic editorial pairing. Riis's site might call for a 19th-century slab serif. Yours might call for something else entirely.
- **Color.** Pick a palette that fits the subject. Update the CSS variables in `src/app.css` and watch the whole site shift.
- **Layout.** The Profile photo could go to the side instead of below the name. The clip grid could be one column instead of three. The back-link could be replaced with a more prominent nav.
- **Detail.** A subtle hover state on the cards. A custom favicon. A footer with a fake masthead.

This is a good place to lean on your AI coding assistant. Try prompts like "Change the color scheme to feel like a 19th-century newspaper" or "Make the clip cards feel more like magazine covers." Iterate until the site feels intentional.

### Task 3: Prepare to present

Be ready to walk the class through your portfolio. You should be prepared to:

- Show the site on a projector or shared screen
- Explain one design choice you made and why
- Tell us who the site belongs to and what that decision changed about your approach
- Tell us what you'd add or change if you had another week
